3. Proposed Application Domain

Domain: Neural Processing Unit (NPU)
Target Task: Efficient and Fast execution of AI Models on edge devices.

4. Problem Description and Rationale
Standard General Purpose Processors (CPUs) face significant bottlenecks when running Deep Learning workloads due to the sequential nature of scalar instructions. A simple matrix multiplication on a standard RISC-V core requires nested loops containing hundreds of LOAD, MUL, and ADD instructions, resulting in high instruction fetch overhead and slow calculations.
On the other hand, GPUs, although very fast, are still General Purpose and not energy efficient, so it is not feasible to run them on edge device.
Our Application Specific Processor (ASP) addresses this by introducing an "NPU" extension. By offloading heavy linear algebra tasks (like Matrix Multiplication) to a dedicated NPU, we can perform 4 multiply-accumulate operations (and more if we have more MAC Units) per single instruction cycle. This design makes specialized hardware to run functions and calculations (like Matrix Multiplication) related to Neural Networks extremely fast, leading to great speed improvements and higher efficiency (because of highly specialized hardware)
In fact, many companies like Apple and Qualcomm now ship dedicated NPU Units with their SOCs, which have enabled AI Computations to run extremely fast on Low Power devices like Mobile Phones.

## A. ISA Specification

**Custom Opcode:** 1110111 (0x77) is used for all NPU instructions to separate them from standard RISC-V operations.

| Instruction | Opcode | Format | Description | Example Usage |
|-------------|--------|--------|-------------|---------------|
| **VLOAD** | 1110111 | I-Type | Load 128-bits (4 integers) from memory into a vector register. | `VLOAD V1, 0(x10)` |
| **VSTORE** | 1110111 | S-Type | Store 128-bits from a vector register into memory. | `VSTORE V1, 0(x10)` |
| **VLBC** | 1110111 | I-Type | Broadcast Load. Read 1 integer from memory and copy it to all 4 slots of the vector register. | `VLBC V2, 4(x10)` |
| **VADD** | 1110111 | R-Type | Vector Addition (V_d = V_s1 + V_s2). | `VADD V3, V1, V2` |
| **VMUL** | 1110111 | R-Type | Vector Multiplication (V_d = V_s1 * V_s2). | `VMUL V3, V1, V2` |
| **VMAC** | 1110111 | R-Type | Multiply-Accumulate. (V_d += V_s1 * V_s2). | `VMAC V0, V1, V2` |
| **VRELU** | 1110111 | R-Type | ReLU Activation. Set any negative value in the vector to 0. | `VRELU V1, V1` |
| **VCLP** | 1110111 | I-Type | Clamp. Cap all values in the vector at a max immediate value (e.g., 255). | `VCLP V1, V1, 255` |
| **VREDMAX** | 1110111 | R-Type | Reduce Max. Find the maximum value in a vector and write it to a scalar register. | `VREDMAX x5, V1` |
| **VARGMAX** | 1110111 | R-Type | ArgMax. Find the index (0-3) of the maximum value and write it to a scalar register. (Helps in classification) | `VARGMAX x5, V1` |
| **VCLR** | 1110111 | R-Type | Clear all elements of a vector register to 0. | `VCLR V0` |

