## D. Expected Performance Analysis

To justify the hardware cost of the Baby NPU, we have performed a theoretical cycle-count analysis comparing a standard RISC-V CPU against our Heterogeneous NPU Architecture for a key kernel: **4-Element Dot Product** (the fundamental operation of Neural Networks).

### 1. Baseline: Standard RISC-V CPU

Performing a dot product of two 4-element vectors (A · B) on a scalar CPU requires a loop.

- **Operations:** 8 Loads (32-bit), 4 Multiplies, 4 Adds, plus loop control overhead (Branch/Increment).
- **Instruction Count:** ~20-25 instructions.
- **Throughput:** 1 scalar operation per cycle.
- **Memory Overhead:** 8 separate memory requests.

### 2. Proposed Solution: Baby NPU

Using our custom ISA, the same operation is vectorised.

- **Code:** VLOAD V1, VLOAD V2, VMAC V0, V1, V2.
- **Instruction Count:** 3 instructions.
- **Throughput:** 4 parallel MAC operations per cycle (SIMD).
- **Memory Overhead:** 2 memory requests (128-bit burst loads).

### 3. Projected Improvements

| Metric | Standard RISC-V CPU | Baby NPU (Ours) | Improvement Factor |
|--------|---------------------|-----------------|-------------------|
| **Instruction Count** | ~24 Instructions | 3 Instructions | **8x Reduction** (Fetch Efficiency) |
| **Compute Cycles** | ~24 Cycles | ~4 Cycles | **6x Speedup** (Latency) |
| **Memory Accesses** | 8 x 32-bit Reads | 2 x 128-bit Reads | **75% Less Bus Contention** |
| **Arithmetic Density** | 0.33 Ops / Cycle | 4.0 Ops / Cycle | **12x Higher Density** |

**Note:** These figures are theoretical estimates based on a 5-stage pipeline model. Actual speedup will be verified in Phase 2 simulations.

