## E. Conclusion

This project aims to bridge the gap between general-purpose computing and the demands of modern AI workloads. By augmenting a standard RISC-V pipeline with a domain-specific **Neural Processing Unit (NPU)**, we address the critical bottlenecks of instruction fetch latency and memory bandwidth.

Our design introduces a specialized ISA capable of **Single-Instruction Multiple-Data (SIMD)** execution, enabling the processor to handle matrix operations, activation functions (ReLU), and classification tasks (ArgMax) with orders of magnitude greater efficiency than a scalar CPU. The introduction of the VLBC (Broadcast) instruction further optimizes the system for multi-layer networks by eliminating CPU-bound data shuffling.

Ultimately, this **Heterogeneous Architecture** demonstrates how dedicated hardware accelerators can enable complex Deep Learning inference on low-power, resource-constrained edge devices.